# Make an Agent Durable

## Overview

A live Agent keeps its committed state in memory. When the Server stops, the
state goes with it. This Livebook adds a persistence adapter to a Jido
instance, starts a counter that saves every commit, stops it, and restores it
from storage with the same state and revision.

The Livebook uses `Jido.Persistence.ETS`. It keeps records for as long as the
BEAM runs, so a restore works after a stop but not after a restart. For storage
that survives a restart, see [Persistence Adapters](persistence-adapters.livemd).

Run the cells from top to bottom.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

## Define the Counter

```elixir
defmodule Durable.Add do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "add",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(%{amount: amount}, %{agent_state: state}) do
    {:ok, %{state | count: state.count + amount}}
  end
end

defmodule Durable.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "counter"

  state do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/guides/durable")
    route "counter.add", Durable.Add, as: :add, defaults: %{amount: 1} do
      state from: output()
    end
  end
end
```

Nothing in the Agent mentions storage. Persistence is a property of the
running instance, not of the definition.

## Configure the Instance

```elixir
defmodule Durable.Jido do
  use Jido,
    otp_app: :guides,
    namespace: "guides/durable",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :guide_agents}
end

{:ok, _sup} = Durable.Jido.start_link()
```

The result is `{:ok, pid}` for the instance supervisor.

| Option | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `namespace:` | Required for persistence. Part of every record key: `{namespace, partition, id}`. Keep it stable across releases. |
| `persistence:` | The instance adapter. `{module, opts}` or a module. `:table` names the ETS table; the default is `:jido_persistence`. |

The instance adapter is a default to select, not a switch. Agents still start
with `persistence: false` unless you ask for it.

## Start a Durable Agent

```elixir
{:ok, counter} =
  Durable.Jido.start_agent(Durable.Counter,
    id: "counter-1",
    persistence: :inherit
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(counter)
Jido.AgentServer.status(counter).state_version
```

The result is `0`. `persistence: :inherit` selects the instance adapter. The
default `persistence_mode: :create` wrote revision 0 before the activation
reported ready.

`start_agent/2` returns `{:ok, pid}` before that write. Call
`Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` before you rely on the record. A failed write
makes `await_ready/2` exit.

Read the stored record directly:

```elixir
{:ok, stored} = Jido.Persistence.load_agent(Durable.Jido, Durable.Counter, "counter-1")
stored.state
```

The result is `%{count: 0}`.

## Commit and Save

```elixir
signal = Durable.Counter.add_signal!(%{amount: 5})
{:ok, agent, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(counter, signal)

{agent.state, Jido.AgentServer.status(counter).state_version}
```

The result is `{%{count: 5}, 1}`. The Server saved revision 1 before it
replied, so the stored record already matches:

```elixir
{:ok, stored} = Jido.Persistence.load_agent(Durable.Jido, Durable.Counter, "counter-1")
stored.state
```

The result is `%{count: 5}`.

Each successful Turn writes one new revision, even when the state does not
change. A failed Turn writes nothing.

## Stop and Restore

```elixir
:ok = Durable.Jido.stop_agent("counter-1")

{:ok, restored} =
  Durable.Jido.start_agent(Durable.Counter,
    id: "counter-1",
    persistence: :inherit,
    persistence_mode: :restore
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(restored)
agent = Jido.AgentServer.agent(restored)
{agent.state, Jido.AgentServer.status(restored).state_version}
```

The result is `{%{count: 5}, 1}`. `:restore` loads the record and continues
from its revision. The next commit writes revision 2.

`Durable.Jido.thaw(Durable.Counter, "counter-1")` is the short form. It sets
`persistence: :inherit` and `persistence_mode: :restore` for you.
[Durable Activation](durable-agent-activation.livemd) covers it with
hibernate.

## Create Twice

`:create` refuses to overwrite an existing record. Try it on a stopped Agent:

```elixir
:ok = Durable.Jido.stop_agent("counter-1")

{:ok, duplicate} =
  Durable.Jido.start_agent(Durable.Counter,
    id: "counter-1",
    persistence: :inherit
  )

try do
  Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(duplicate)
catch
  :exit, reason -> reason
end
```

The result is an exit tuple whose first element is `{:shutdown, :conflict}`.
The activation stopped before it accepted a Signal. The stored record is
unchanged at revision 1.

Startup failures follow this pattern: `start_agent/2` returns `{:ok, pid}`,
then `await_ready/2` exits with the reason.

| Exit reason | Cause |
| --- | --- |
| `{:shutdown, :conflict}` | `:create` found an existing record. |
| `{:shutdown, :not_found}` | `:restore` found no record. |
| `{:shutdown, :deleted}` | `:restore` found a tombstone. |
| `{:shutdown, :stable_namespace_required}` | The instance has no `namespace:`. |
| `{:shutdown, :incompatible_durable_checkpoint}` | `:inherit` with no instance adapter. |

## Choose an Adapter

You select the adapter at start:

| `persistence:` | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `false` | The default. No records. |
| `:inherit` | The instance adapter. |
| `Module` or `{Module, opts}` | This adapter, for this Agent only. |

An explicit adapter overrides the instance adapter:

```elixir
{:ok, other} =
  Durable.Jido.start_agent(Durable.Counter,
    id: "counter-2",
    persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :guide_other},
    persistence_timeout: 2_000
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(other)

{:ok, stored} =
  Jido.Persistence.load_agent(
    {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :guide_other},
    Durable.Counter,
    "counter-2",
    namespace: "guides/durable"
  )

stored.state
```

The result is `%{count: 0}`. The record is in `:guide_other`, not in the
instance table.

`persistence_timeout:` (default 5,000 ms) limits each storage operation. A
save that does not finish in time stops the activation and reports an unknown
outcome, because the remote write can still land.

To make every Agent on an instance durable, start the instance with an Agent
Server default: `Durable.Jido.start_link(agent_server: [persistence: :inherit])`.
Agents then use the instance adapter unless they pass `persistence: false`.

## Stop the Example

```elixir
:ok = Durable.Jido.stop_agent("counter-2")
:ok = Jido.stop(Durable.Jido)
```

ETS records stay until the BEAM stops.

